# L §4.3 rule 1 (writer 6): law-dated is "one with a row or version whose span isn't always"; an input with no row
# at all has none, so a def reading it needs no `at` (rule 2), and no LOAD-LAWDATE. Reading it, L §4.6 step 5: no
# candidate is HOLE-UNGOVERNED, its question the input's `question` (q2: k's "Ile?"); without one, read with no law
# date, "the question ends at no value: ... x: no value" (L §4.6 step 5; q1 through the def f, q3 directly). h's rules
# point at versions that govern always: more beats base, h = 1.
=== nr.tally
model nr
  tally 2
  on 2026-10-25
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "synthetic"

input n: int
input k: int question "Ile?"
input r: int reading

def f: int = n + 1
def g: int = k + 1
def h: int
  rule base v v1 = 0
  rule more beats base v v2 = 1

ask q1: f
ask q2: g
ask q3: n
ask q4: h

version v1
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01

version v2
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-02
=== expect
q1: ?"n: no value" (n)
  cut 2026-10-25
q2: ?"Ile?" (k)
  cut 2026-10-25
q3: ?"n: no value" (n)
  cut 2026-10-25
q4: 1
  cut 2026-10-25
